---
/**
 * The corrections policy, and the dated log it commits to.
 *
 * Eight entries, newest first, each one an error that moved a recommendation rather
 * than a decimal. The narrative versions of several of these also appear on
 * `/how-sure/`, which reached them from the confidence vocabulary; this page is the
 * dated record with permanent anchors, and it owns the policy.
 *
 * Every date is the day the corrected figure landed in the repository, which is
 * checkable in the commit history. Do not invent one for an entry whose date cannot be
 * established: leave the entry off instead.
 */
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import MarginNote from "~/components/MarginNote.astro";
import Rail from "~/components/Rail.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import { REPO_URL } from "~/lib/site";
---

<Base
  title="Corrections"
  description="What this project published, what turned out to be wrong with it, what caught the error, and what changed. Eight dated entries, including a return figure that was never measured and was deleted rather than replaced with a better guess."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <Rail>Corrections</Rail>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">Corrections</h1>

    <MarginNote label="The question">
      <p>
        What have you published that turned out to be wrong? Dates are the day the corrected figure landed in
        <a href={REPO_URL} rel="noopener noreferrer">the repository</a>.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Thirty-two errors have been found in this project's own published numbers. Eight are below, chosen because
    each moved a recommendation rather than a decimal.</p>

    <p>If you read one, read <a href="#trend-premium">the trend-following return</a>. It was quoted confidently on
    this site for most of the project's life, nobody had ever measured it, it had three separate errors stacked
    inside it, and when we worked out what it actually was we deleted it instead of printing a better guess.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Policy</Rail>

    <h2 id="policy">What happens when we find one</h2>

    <p>Six rules, written down before they were needed and revised twice since.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Why not just edit it">
      <p>
        A site that quietly fixes its numbers is indistinguishable from a site that was right all along, which is
        the impression a reader has no way to check.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>The number is corrected everywhere it appears, and an entry lands here with its date. We do not edit a
    figure and say nothing.</p>

    <p>A frozen specification is never edited, even when it is the thing that was wrong. Its defect is recorded on
    the page that reads it, and the original keeps its own hash and its own verdict, because a specification you
    can revise after seeing its result is worth nothing as a specification.</p>

    <p>A withdrawn number is deleted rather than replaced with a better guess. Where we do not know, the page says
    so and prints nothing in the gap. This is harder than it sounds, because a blank looks like an oversight and a
    plausible number looks like work.</p>

    <p>Where a correction has a mechanical fix, the fix is code that refuses rather than a note asking future
    readers to remember. Three of the entries below ended with a function raising an exception on the exact
    mistake that produced them.</p>

    <p>Typos, broken links and clumsy sentences are fixed silently. An entry appears here when an error moved a
    number, a verdict or a recommendation.</p>

    <p>The other twenty-four corrections live on the research page that owns the number, each beside the figure it
    changed, along with the commit that made the change. They are smaller, and they are all in the same
    repository.</p>

    <Callout kind="do-this">
      <p>
        Found one we missed? <a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues`} rel="noopener noreferrer">Open an issue</a>. A number
        with its source and the sentence you think it contradicts is enough; you do not need to be polite about
        it.
      </p>
    </Callout>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The log</Rail>

    <h2 id="log">The log</h2>

    <p>Newest first.</p>

    <h3 id="two-horizons">"How long before you would know" was two questions wearing one label</h3>

    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>Corrected 2026-08-23</p>

    <p><strong>What we believed.</strong> That the site could put about three and a half months for the fee and tax savings
    beside <span data-numeric>59</span> years for the whole construction and call it one comparison. It was the
    site's signature line and it appeared on the home page.</p>

    <p><strong>What was wrong.</strong> The two numbers answer different questions. Four months comes from
    <code>T = (z·s/e)²</code>, which is when your own account would separate an effect from the thing you are
    comparing it against. The 59 years came from scaling a design's smallest visible effect across its 427-month
    panel, which is when a study could separate that effect from zero. Different question, different arithmetic,
    one label on top of both.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Both are still published">
      <p>
        Neither number was wrong. What was wrong was contrasting them with each other, which is why both survive
        and each one now says which question it answers.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p><strong>What caught it.</strong> Writing every horizon on the site into one table, in order to build the
    figure records, and finding two that could not both be following the same rule.</p>

    <p><strong>What changed.</strong> The headline contrast is now three and a half months against <span data-numeric>31</span>
    years, both under the first rule. The construction's own-account horizon is
    <Figure id="time-to-know-construction" inline showUnit={false} />, not 59. The
    <Figure id="detection-horizon-construction" inline showUnit={false} /> survives on
    <a href="/how-sure/">how sure we are</a>, labelled as the second question and never contrasted with the first.
    Two records carrying 59 years under an own-account heading were withdrawn.</p>

    <h3 id="tracking-error">One name holding two different facts</h3>

    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>Corrected 2026-08-23</p>

    <p><strong>What we believed.</strong> That <code>tracking-error</code> named one quantity.</p>

    <p><strong>What was wrong.</strong> It named two. Six percent is how far the portfolio drifts from a
    borrowing-matched control in a typical year. Four hundred basis points is how far it drifts from a cheap index
    fund holding the same split. Both are real, both are published, and any sentence reaching for the identifier
    got whichever one had been written most recently.</p>

    <p><strong>What caught it.</strong> A checker that walks every figure record and refuses to let one identifier
    carry two different facts. It was written for a different reason and found this on its first run.</p>

    <p><strong>What changed.</strong> Split in two, with the comparison named in each record. The checker runs in
    the build, so the collision cannot come back.</p>

    <h3 id="trend-premium">A trend-following return we had never measured</h3>

    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>Corrected 2026-08-22</p>

    <MarginNote label="Source">
      <p>
        <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/docs/research/adversarial-review.md`} rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >Adversarial review of the August 2026 construction session</a
        >, as of <span data-numeric>2026-08-22</span>.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p><strong>What we believed.</strong> That trend following would return
    <Figure id="trend-premium-withdrawn" inline showUnit={false} /> percentage points a year from here. The figure
    sized a position, and then it was used to argue against holding that position at all.</p>

    <p><strong>What was wrong.</strong> Nobody had measured it. It was the 2012 to 2025 stretch's own compounded
    average with a 1.50% fee taken off, and it was then compared against a break-even computed on a completely
    different basis, with the same fee charged a second time by the model that consumed it. Three errors inside
    one number: a variance term worth 0.77 points, a fee counted twice, and a point estimate used with no range
    when its own 95% range ran from −2.67 to +11.00 and therefore contained the 10.98 it was being used to
    overturn.</p>

    <p><strong>What caught it.</strong> Running the arithmetic backwards. Add the fee back and add the variance
    term back and 1.80 returns 4.07, which is that same file's own mean on the basis the break-even used. A
    forecast that reconstructs an in-sample average to the second decimal is not a forecast.</p>

    <p><strong>What changed.</strong> The sign of the recommendation. On the published basis the position
    subtracted 0.35 percentage points a year against the same portfolio without it; corrected, it adds 0.34
    against that same comparison. The recommended weight moved to <span data-numeric>25%</span>, and the modelled
    chance of giving up on it at a <span data-numeric>30%</span> weight fell to
    <span data-numeric>17.2%</span>.</p>

    <p>And the number itself is gone. Nothing replaced it, because the defensible estimates run
    <span data-numeric>2.84</span> to <span data-numeric>7.18</span> percentage points a year depending on which
    construction you believe, the break-even is <span data-numeric>2.96</span>, and picking one out of that spread
    would be choosing the answer rather than measuring it.</p>

    <h3 id="borrowed-comparison">A borrowed portfolio compared against an index that borrowed nothing</h3>

    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>Corrected 2026-08-22</p>

    <p><strong>What we believed.</strong> That the construction beat the index, so the construction was doing
    something.</p>

    <p><strong>What was wrong.</strong> The construction holds $1.32 of assets for every dollar put into it and
    the index holds $1.00. You beat that index in good years and trail it in bad ones whatever else you do. Take
    the credit on the way up and you will take the blame on the way down, and you will be wrong both times.</p>

    <MarginNote label="What matched exposure showed">
      <p>
        Sharpe over the same window: the trend route rose 0.538 to 0.641 while the borrowed-equity route fell to
        0.530. A gross exposure figure cannot tell the two routes apart, which is the whole point.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p><strong>What caught it.</strong> Building a control that borrows exactly as much as the portfolio does, and
    running everything again against it.</p>

    <p><strong>What changed.</strong> At matched exposure the worst loss was
    <Figure id="levered-comparison-drawdown" inline showUnit={false} />. The consequence is printed even though it
    is unflattering: the single whole-portfolio comparison in the entire programme that clears its own resolution,
    the recommended portfolio against the investor's original at −0.50 percentage points a year, is mostly
    measuring 5.4 points of borrowing rather than anything about how the portfolio is built.</p>

    <h3 id="mismatched-inference">A visibility bar on one statistical basis, printed beside a range on another</h3>

    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>Corrected 2026-08-22</p>

    <p><strong>What we believed.</strong> That the strongest measurement on the site cleared the smallest
    difference its test could see by 68%.</p>

    <p><strong>What was wrong.</strong> The range had been computed with error bars that allow for markets
    clustering, calm with calm and panic with panic. The bar printed beside it had been computed with error bars
    that treat every month as a fresh coin flip. Two bases, one sentence.</p>

    <p><strong>What caught it.</strong> Reading the run's own output file rather than the page. Both numbers were
    in it. Only the narrower one had been published.</p>

    <p><strong>What changed.</strong> On matched error bars the bar is
    <Figure id="detection-floor-restated" inline showUnit={false} /> percentage points a year rather than 0.47.
    The result still clears, by <span data-numeric>10%</span> rather than 68%. The data a study would need before
    it could separate the effect from zero goes to <Figure id="tilt-horizon" inline showUnit={false} />. The
    implied signal-to-noise ratio drops 4.73 to 3.05, which lands exactly on the published hurdle for a result
    that was found by searching. One arm of the tournament stops clearing altogether.</p>

    <h3 id="cross-benchmark-sum">Adding up savings measured against different comparisons</h3>

    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>Corrected 2026-08-22</p>

    <p><strong>What we believed.</strong> That putting each fund in the account that taxes it least was worth +38
    to +55 basis points a year. It was the largest new number in the recommendation.</p>

    <p><strong>What was wrong.</strong> Four things at once. The range had been truncated, dropping the entire
    distributed reading, worth 18 basis points. A rebalancing hurdle avoided had been booked as a saving, worth
    14, on the same page that says a hurdle avoided is not one. Choosing which shares to sell and never selling at
    all had both been booked, and each one excludes the other. And 94.7% of the top figure came from a single
    holding's undistributed foreign accrual.</p>

    <MarginNote label="The structural error">
      <p>
        Underneath all four: some lines were measured against a cheap index fund and some against the portfolio
        you would otherwise own. A cheap index, your own counterfactual and the average investor are three
        different questions, and their answers never add.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p><strong>What caught it.</strong> An independent re-derivation of the arithmetic, which reproduced every
    priority, the credit cap and all twelve cells exactly. The calculation was right. What was booked from it was
    not.</p>

    <p><strong>What changed.</strong> The line reads <Figure id="placement-restated" inline showUnit={false} />
    basis points a year against a control an investor could actually hold, with the conditional +19 to +49
    reported beside it and added to nothing. The aggregating function raises an exception on a cross-benchmark
    sum, and the function that reports one component of the placement result refuses to return a negative value
    and says so in the exception.</p>

    <h3 id="qualified-dividends">Assuming every dividend was the good kind</h3>

    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>Corrected 2026-08-22</p>

    <p><strong>What we believed.</strong> That fund dividends qualify for the long-term rate. It is the standard
    simplifying assumption and nobody had checked it.</p>

    <p><strong>What was wrong.</strong> It had reversed the account-placement ranking for emerging-market stocks,
    which is exactly the decision the section existed to make.</p>

    <p><strong>What caught it.</strong> Reading the sponsors' own filed fractions, which took an afternoon. VEA
    files 66.27%. VWO files <Figure id="qualified-fraction-vwo" inline showUnit={false} />. IEMG 34.82%, AVES
    44.48%, IDMO 25%, DFIV 100%.</p>

    <p><strong>What changed.</strong> Every placement conclusion that predated the filed fractions had to be
    worked again rather than restated, which is slow and unpleasant and the only honest option. A placement
    ranking that assumes a qualified fraction has assumed its own answer.</p>

    <h3 id="capture-double-count">Two measurements of the same thing, multiplied together</h3>

    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>Corrected 2026-08-17</p>

    <p><strong>What we believed.</strong> That a fund's measured value exposure of 0.410 should be discounted by a
    long-only capture fraction of 0.520, giving
    <Figure id="capture-double-count" inline showUnit={false} />. That product was used in five places.</p>

    <p><strong>What was wrong.</strong> The two numbers measure the same exposure, so multiplying them discounts
    it twice. Work through the algebra and the capture fraction turns out to be the exposure plus a small
    residual, exact to sixteen decimal places on our own files: 94% of the measured 0.520 is the 0.4891 coming
    back around. The product also sits below the measured exposure of every audited value product on the shelf,
    which should have been the tell and was not.</p>

    <p><strong>What caught it.</strong> Deriving the identity between the two quantities instead of accepting two
    published numbers as independent.</p>

    <p><strong>What changed.</strong> The value line had been understated by roughly a factor of two. The chain is
    three terms now: weight, times the difference in exposure between the fund and the thing it replaces, times
    the return, minus cost. The research code raises <code>CaptureDoubleCountError</code> rather than accepting a
    capture argument, so nobody can do it by hand again. The 0.40 sitting in the edge budget was deleted rather
    than replaced.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The pattern</Rail>

    <h2 id="pattern">What the eight have in common</h2>

    <p>Six were caught by the project arguing with itself: an adversarial review, a re-derivation of arithmetic
    somebody had already done once, a control built to have a known answer. One was caught by reading a filing
    instead of a summary. One was caught by a build check that had been written for something else.</p>

    <p>Seven of the eight were arithmetic that was correct when somebody did it. What broke was what happened
    afterwards, when the number left the page that made it true and travelled without its window, its comparison
    or its error bars. That is the failure this site's whole content model is built against, and it is why every
    number here drags its source along behind it.</p>

    <p>None of that means the current numbers are right.</p>

  </div>
</Base>
